1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,160
If you want to master UX design,

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:03,860
you have to start with the fundamentals.

3
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:06,680
So, let's start from the very beginning.

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,400
This is easiest to explain with an example.

5
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:13,260
Let's say I have three groups of text and icons.

6
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:15,860
But what if I add a container around both the drinks and the food?

7
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:17,440
you'll know they're related.

8
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:18,620
while dessert is not.

9
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:21,710
Now, what if I only add a container around the drinks?

10
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:23,210
you'll know it's been selected.

11
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:24,470
Or you can switch to the actual product.

12
00:00:24,470 --> 00:00:26,610
If I were to change this text to gray,

13
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:28,810
you'll know it's disabled.

14
00:00:28,810 --> 00:00:31,230
Clicking on it probably won't do anything.

15
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:34,350
See? How you understood all of that in an instant.

16
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:36,490
I don't even need to write any instructions.

17
00:00:36,490 --> 00:00:39,360
That's because the UI itself is already communicating all of that.

18
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:41,580
A great UI is full of these kinds of cues.

19
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:43,480
For example, the state of a button when it's pressed,

20
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:46,300
or the highlight and hover states for active items in the navigation bar.

21
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,120
There are even tooltips to show users what the UI can do.

22
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:51,940
or what it does.

23
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,120
Now, let's take a look at this card.

24
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:57,560
It's laid out in a clear, logical way,

25
00:00:57,560 --> 00:01:00,400
A repeatable format displays all the information.

26
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:02,680
But it looks more like a spreadsheet.

27
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:04,180
and not a piece of design.

28
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:06,920
So, let's give it some hierarchy.

29
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:08,360
This is actually pretty intuitive.

30
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,660
We can use size, position, and color to our advantage.

31
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:12,920
Let's start from the top.

32
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:15,500
An image always brings with it vivid colors.

33
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:17,460
and makes browsing a breeze.

34
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:18,620
Just like how UBER does it.

35
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,020
So, in a situation like this,

36
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:21,260
The items themselves are the most important thing.

37
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:23,020
we need to make it big and eye-catching.

38
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:24,520
And it should be placed at the top.

39
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:28,479
Otherwise, it'll just get jumbled up with information like the time and date.

40
00:01:28,479 --> 00:01:30,539
And this other information can be placed down below.

41
00:01:30,539 --> 00:01:32,119
And the font size should be much smaller.

42
00:01:32,119 --> 00:01:33,039
And it's this contrast,

43
00:01:33,039 --> 00:01:34,260
For instance, differences in size,

44
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:36,200
or the difference between elements with color and those without.

45
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:37,620
This is what actually creates a sense of hierarchy.

46
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,040
And that's why the price should be placed at the top.

47
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:41,020
and right-aligned.

48
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:43,980
And we'll make it blue to show that it's different from the other content.

49
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:45,660
This way, it will catch people's attention.

50
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:47,040
The location information should be smaller.

51
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:48,240
and place it at the bottom.

52
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:48,960
Just like this.

53
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,840
But we don't have to limit ourselves to just the start and end points.

54
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:53,440
The text can be represented by an icon and a line.

55
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:56,280
This way, you can show the movement from Jamesville to Syracuse.

56
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:57,880
without having to state it directly.

57
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:00,620
Of course, it's not an exact science.

58
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:03,700
You can absolutely design different styles just like these.

59
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:04,660
And there's nothing wrong with that.

60
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:06,760
But the same principles still apply.

61
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:08,780
Put the most important information at the top.

62
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:10,900
The larger and more vibrant the content, the more important it is.

63
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,740
And whenever possible, use images.

64
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:14,400
As you design more and more,

65
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,200
You'll find that when you need labels or buttons,

66
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:19,040
similar design patterns will emerge.

67
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:21,700
We'll get into that in a moment.

68
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:28,540
Now, some people get the wrong idea and think that everything has to be aligned to a 12-column grid.

69
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:31,350
and the spacing has to be exactly eight pixels.

70
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:36,930
Take this design, for example. It's not aligned to any of the columns.

71
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:38,480
And that's perfectly fine.

72
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,540
This is especially true for custom landing pages.

73
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:42,860
But for pages with a highly consistent structure,

74
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:46,249
like galleries, blogs, or any page with repeating content.

75
00:02:46,249 --> 00:02:50,980
In fact, grids can be incredibly useful by providing a framework for responsive behavior.

76
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:52,620
use an eight-column grid on tablets,

77
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:54,590
and four columns for mobile.

78
00:02:54,590 --> 00:02:58,570
But even more important than overusing grids and layouts is whitespace.

79
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:00,450
Give your elements room to breathe.

80
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:03,340
For instance, in a simple block like this one,

81
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:06,490
The font size and line height for the main heading will look something like this.

82
00:03:06,490 --> 00:03:09,330
while the subheading has its own font size and line height.

83
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:11,420
And it's the same for buttons.

84
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:14,600
You can also choose to add a top announcement bar like this one.

85
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:18,710
Then, just make sure to leave a 32-pixel gap between each element.

86
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:22,310
But you can also group together elements that naturally belong together.

87
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:25,270
For instance, an announcement and its text, or body text and its subtext.

88
00:03:25,270 --> 00:03:27,820
And this is actually another way to establish a visual hierarchy.

89
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:31,500
And this is where we see the four-point grid system really come into play.

90
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,520
All the spacing is a multiple of something.

91
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,840
And it's not just because it looks better.

92
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,280
but because it allows you to always split the spacing in half.

93
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:41,640
This helps keep the entire design consistent.

94
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,270
Typography is actually a lot of fun.

95
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:47,090
Because design is mostly text.

96
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:48,550
And when it comes to choosing a font,

97
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:50,290
And I can say this with almost absolute certainty:

98
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:53,450
you don't need more than one typeface for any design.

99
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:56,090
So, just find a good-looking sans-serif font,

100
00:03:56,090 --> 00:03:57,430
Like the ones you see on screen right now.

101
00:03:57,430 --> 00:03:58,930
And just stick with it.

102
00:03:58,930 --> 00:04:00,709
In my seven years of working in design,

103
00:04:00,709 --> 00:04:02,389
Besides the fonts you see on screen,

104
00:04:02,389 --> 00:04:04,009
I've barely used any other typefaces.

105
00:04:04,009 --> 00:04:06,369
And you shouldn't spend too much time on it either.

106
00:04:06,369 --> 00:04:10,040
To be honest, I'm not a big fan of calling any design skill a 'trick'.

107
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:12,510
But this method does have a bit of that design feel to it.

108
00:04:12,510 --> 00:04:13,870
It looks okay for now.

109
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:16,850
But our heading text feels a little too loose.

110
00:04:16,850 --> 00:04:18,649
If you tighten up the letter spacing,

111
00:04:18,649 --> 00:04:21,110
by about 2% to 3%.

112
00:04:21,110 --> 00:04:25,570
and then reduce the line height to around 110% to 120%.

113
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:28,610
Any large text will instantly look very professional.

114
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:32,070
However, the hierarchy in typography is a bit more subtle.

115
00:04:32,070 --> 00:04:34,010
If we look back at our previous example,

116
00:04:34,010 --> 00:04:36,350
The font sizes for your text can vary quite a bit.

117
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:38,270
For landing pages and websites,

118
00:04:38,270 --> 00:04:41,070
Generally, it's not recommended to use more than six different font sizes.

119
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:43,290
And the range between these six sizes can be quite wide.

120
00:04:43,290 --> 00:04:45,730
But for interfaces like dashboards,

121
00:04:45,730 --> 00:04:47,670
that range will be much smaller.

122
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:51,090
You usually won't find font sizes larger than 24 pixels.

123
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:52,970
This is because the information density is higher.

124
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:54,090
But to be honest,

125
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:59,150
One of the most important principles in design is to draw inspiration from good sources and conduct research.

126
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:00,770
Like today's sponsor, MOBAN.

127
00:05:00,770 --> 00:05:03,370
They've handpicked hundreds of thousands of mobile and web apps,

128
00:05:03,370 --> 00:05:04,290
and landing pages.

129
00:05:04,290 --> 00:05:09,060
This way, you can draw inspiration from real-world designs crafted by the world's top design teams anytime you want.

130
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:11,500
If you're struggling with a specific section,

131
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:12,700
Just do a quick search,

132
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:14,730
and instantly find real-world examples.

133
00:05:14,730 --> 00:05:17,490
They even come with links that take you to the actual websites.

134
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:18,630
If you're interested,

135
00:05:18,630 --> 00:05:21,190
You can access MOBY through the first link down below.

136
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:22,330
and get a 20% discount.

137
00:05:22,330 --> 00:05:25,330
Alright, let's get back to the video.

138
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:29,020
Color is tricky to get right.

139
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:30,800
Plus, everyone has their own preferences.

140
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:33,280
But personally, I love to start with one primary color.

141
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:35,630
And this is usually your brand color.

142
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:36,790
Then, you can brighten it up,

143
00:05:36,790 --> 00:05:39,710
use it as a background color, or darken it for text.

144
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:42,460
Both of these methods allow you to subtly incorporate nuanced colors.

145
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:44,860
This makes a design that was once bland much more interesting.

146
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:45,980
Once you've done all that,

147
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:48,580
we've actually already built half of our color scale.

148
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:52,500
And this color hierarchy is precisely how large companies design the states of their color blocks,

149
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:54,840
charts, and the foundation for all sorts of other elements.

150
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:55,780
But I also think,

151
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:56,820
As a beginner,

152
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:59,700
It's also important to let colors find you.

153
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:01,960
For example, using a bulletin board to draw attention.

154
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:04,180
Or, by adding a focus state to an input field.

155
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:06,720
Or, you could add a green 'new' tag to a link.

156
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:08,060
These are all examples of semantic colors.

157
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:09,720
In other words, they have a specific meaning.

158
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:11,600
Colors that can provide guidance to the user.

159
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:13,000
Blue represents trust.

160
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,220
Red signifies danger or an emergency.

161
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:16,720
Yellow represents a warning.

162
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:18,240
And green represents success.

163
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:21,320
Always make sure you use color with a purpose.

164
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,820
and not just for decoration.

165
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:28,940
Now, let's talk about designing in dark mode.

166
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:30,669
and that brings some very interesting challenges.

167
00:06:30,669 --> 00:06:33,269
First, this card uses a light-colored border.

168
00:06:33,269 --> 00:06:34,930
This makes the contrast a bit too high.

169
00:06:34,930 --> 00:06:36,790
So, we need to tone it down a bit.

170
00:06:36,790 --> 00:06:38,090
In dark mode,

171
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:40,710
Unlike in light mode, we don't have that drop shadow effect.

172
00:06:40,710 --> 00:06:42,570
So, to create a sense of hierarchy,

173
00:06:42,570 --> 00:06:45,130
We need to make the card a bit brighter than the background.

174
00:06:45,130 --> 00:06:46,650
So, I'll make some adjustments accordingly.

175
00:06:46,650 --> 00:06:48,460
And this tag chip is way too bright.

176
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,620
So, we can just turn down the saturation and brightness.

177
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:52,480
And we can apply this same principle of contrast to the text.

178
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:53,840
This creates a sense of hierarchy.

179
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:55,460
Additionally, dark purple,

180
00:06:55,460 --> 00:06:58,320
Reds and greens also offer a lot of flexibility.

181
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:00,240
and it's not just limited to navy blue.

182
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:02,480
blue or gray,

183
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:06,000
But when it comes to creating a sense of hierarchy,

184
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,820
Shadows are a fantastic tool in light mode.

185
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:12,510
This shadow, like most others, is just too intense.

186
00:07:12,510 --> 00:07:15,990
So, lowering the opacity and increasing the blur can be a huge help.

187
00:07:15,990 --> 00:07:19,130
The intensity of the shadow you need depends on the foreground and background.

188
00:07:19,130 --> 00:07:21,610
But for card-like elements, you'll want a much softer shadow.

189
00:07:21,610 --> 00:07:25,030
But for elements like pop-ups, which sit on top of other content,

190
00:07:25,030 --> 00:07:26,440
will require a stronger shadow.

191
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:33,340
This creates effects like raised, tactile buttons.

192
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:34,240
Just remember,

193
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:37,380
If the shadow is the first thing you notice in a design,

194
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:39,840
that means you're doing it wrong.

195
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:44,500
So, to create a powerful design,

196
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:45,940
You're also going to need icons.

197
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:47,960
The problem is, most icons are usually way too big.

198
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:50,760
So, the trick is to get your font's line height.

199
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:52,590
So, for example, let's say this is 24 pixels.

200
00:07:52,590 --> 00:07:54,770
Then, make the icon the same height as the line height.

201
00:07:54,770 --> 00:07:56,490
Then, tighten up the spacing between the text.

202
00:07:56,490 --> 00:07:57,870
Take these sidebar links, for instance.

203
00:07:57,870 --> 00:07:59,260
Basically, it's a button with no background.

204
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:01,080
and the background only appears when you hover over it.

205
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:03,160
This type of button is often called a ghost button.

206
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:05,340
Now, if we take one of these and isolate it,

207
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:06,520
and center it.

208
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:08,320
And you get a perfect, standalone button.

209
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:11,340
Now, when you have a primary and a secondary call-to-action button appearing side-by-side,

210
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:12,940
This is a very common design pattern.

211
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:14,660
Now, about the padding for these buttons.

212
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:21,790
These buttons can also have icons or be icon-less.

213
00:08:23,190 --> 00:08:27,910
A good design principle is that whenever a user performs any action,

214
00:08:27,910 --> 00:08:29,570
the interface should provide corresponding feedback.

215
00:08:29,570 --> 00:08:32,650
For example, every button should have at least four states.

216
00:08:32,650 --> 00:08:34,270
Default, hover,

217
00:08:34,270 --> 00:08:37,208
activated or pressed, and disabled.

218
00:08:37,208 --> 00:08:39,029
Sometimes, you'll also need a loading state.

219
00:08:39,029 --> 00:08:41,889
And when you pair that with a loading animation, the feedback for the input field becomes even more critical.

220
00:08:41,889 --> 00:08:45,350
When a user clicks on an input field, it needs to have a focused state.

221
00:08:45,350 --> 00:08:48,630
If an error occurs, it should be marked with a red border and an error message.

222
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:51,650
Sometimes, you also need to add a warning state for optional questions.

223
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:53,370
And this applies everywhere.

224
00:08:53,370 --> 00:08:56,579
For instance, show a loading animation while data is being loaded.

225
00:08:56,579 --> 00:08:59,059
or a success message once an action is completed.

226
00:08:59,059 --> 00:09:02,279
You can even add subtle animations when scrolling or swiping.

227
00:09:02,279 --> 00:09:05,019
Every interaction needs a feedback.

228
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,220
And this naturally brings us to the topic of micro-interactions.

229
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:12,100
Micro-interactions are a form of feedback.

230
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:14,319
But they can significantly enhance the experience.

231
00:09:14,319 --> 00:09:19,299


232
00:09:19,299 --> 00:09:22,359
But we still don't know if it was actually copied successfully.

233
00:09:22,359 --> 00:09:25,820
But what if, instead, we have this notification bar slide in?

234
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:28,560
This is a microinteraction that confirms our action.

235
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:30,680
These micro-interactions can be incredibly practical.

236
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:33,600
They can also be fun and playful.

237
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:38,860
Finally, overlays are incredibly important.

238
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:40,260
Because if you don't handle it properly,

239
00:09:40,260 --> 00:09:41,780
Not only will the image be ruined,

240
00:09:41,780 --> 00:09:43,140
but the text will suffer as well.

241
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:44,280
So, in a situation like this,

242
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:45,780
But we can't just leave it like this.

243
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:46,160
But either way,

244
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:47,980
We could add a full-screen overlay.

245
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:50,480
But that approach doesn't really do justice to the beauty of the image.

246
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:53,790
So, let's try a different approach and add a linear gradient instead.

247
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:55,070
This way, we can still showcase the image,

248
00:09:55,070 --> 00:09:58,130
while also creating a smooth transition to a background that's easier to read text on.

249
00:09:58,130 --> 00:10:00,650
Or, if we want to make it a bit more flashy,

250
00:10:00,650 --> 00:10:03,750
You can also layer a progressive blur on top of the gradient.

251
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:05,890
to give the whole thing a more modern look.

252
00:10:05,890 --> 00:10:08,710
And that pretty much covers all the fundamental UI/UX concepts.

253
00:10:08,710 --> 00:10:10,410
